window.WB_BOOK=window.WB_BOOK||{};window.WB_BOOK["week16"]={"id":"week16","n":17,"section":"X","label":"Mock Exam 2","title":"Full Paper 01 Mock (60 MCQs) + Final Review","titleText":"Full Paper 01 Mock (60 MCQs) + Final Review","subtitle":"One complete original Paper 01 (60 items, 1 h 15 min), a weak-topic finder, and your day-by-day plan for the last week","intro":"This is your **final mock**. Paper 01 is on **Monday 25 January 2027 at 9:00**. It has **60 multiple-choice items in 1 hour 15 minutes**, covering all five sections. The mock in this book is built exactly like the real paper: 60 original items, in section order A → E, with diagrams. **Do the mock FIRST**, in one sitting, timed, using the answer grid on the next pages. Only then read Unit 16.1, which shows you how to turn your mistakes into a list of weak topics and tells you which workbook to open for each one. Unit 16.2 is your day-by-day plan for 18–25 January.","objectives":[{"code":"A","text":"Section A Mechanics (16 items): measurement, vectors, moments, Hooke's law, motion graphs, Newton, momentum, energy, power, pressure, flotation"},{"code":"B","text":"Section B Thermal physics (10 items): theories of heat, thermometers, kelvin, gas laws, specific and latent heat, evaporation, convection"},{"code":"C","text":"Section C Waves and optics (11 items): wave types and graphs, ⟪v=f\\lambda⟫, sound, EM uses, eclipses, mirrors, TIR, lenses"},{"code":"D","text":"Section D Electricity and magnetism (16 items): static, Q = It, rectification, circuits, kWh, mains safety, logic, magnets, motor, induction, transformers"},{"code":"E","text":"Section E The atom (7 items): atomic models, Geiger–Marsden, atomic structure, beta decay, penetration, half-life, uses"},{"code":"P01","text":"Sit a full Paper 01 in 75 minutes, mark it, find my weak topics and follow a final revision plan"}],"formulaCard":[{"tex":"\\rho =m\\div V","meaning":"density; V of irregular solid = rise in water level","units":"g cm^−3^ or kg m^−3^"},{"tex":"R=\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}","meaning":"resultant of two perpendicular vectors","units":""},{"tex":"F\\times d\\ (\\text{clockwise})=F\\times d\\ (\\text{anticlockwise})","meaning":"principle of moments; d to the pivot","units":"N m"},{"tex":"F=kx","meaning":"Hooke's law; x = extension = length − natural length","units":"N; m or cm"},{"tex":"a=(v-u)\\div t;\\quad \\text{ distance }=\\text{ area under }v\\text{–}t","meaning":"acceleration; gradient of v–t graph","units":"m s^−2^; m"},{"tex":"p=mv;\\quad \\text{ total }p\\text{ before }=\\text{ total }p\\text{ after}","meaning":"momentum and its conservation","units":"kg m s^−1^"},{"tex":"E_{p}=mgh;\\quad E_{k}=\\tfrac{1}{2}mv^{2};\\quad P=E\\div t","meaning":"potential and kinetic energy; power","units":"J; W"},{"tex":"P=F\\div A;\\quad P=\\rho gh","meaning":"pressure; pressure in a fluid","units":"Pa"},{"tex":"T/K=\\theta /{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}+273;\\quad pV\\div T=\\text{ constant}","meaning":"kelvin; general gas law (T in K)","units":"K"},{"tex":"E_{H}=mc\\Delta \\theta ;\\quad E_{H}=ml","meaning":"specific heat capacity; specific latent heat","units":"J"},{"tex":"v=f\\lambda ;\\quad T=1\\div f","meaning":"wave equation; period","units":"m s^−1^; s"},{"tex":"n=\\sin i\\div \\sin r=c\\div v;\\quad \\sin c=1\\div n","meaning":"refractive index; critical angle","units":"no unit"},{"tex":"Q=\\text{ It};\\quad V=IR;\\quad P=IV;\\quad E=Pt","meaning":"charge; Ohm's law; power; energy ⟪(\\mathrm{kWh}=\\mathrm{kW}\\times h)⟫","units":"C; Ω; W; J or kWh"},{"tex":"R=R_{1}+R_{2};\\quad 1\\div R=1\\div R_{1}+1\\div R_{2}","meaning":"series; parallel resistance","units":"Ω"},{"tex":"V_{p}\\div V_{s}=N_{p}\\div N_{s}","meaning":"ideal transformer","units":""},{"tex":"A=Z+N;\\quad \\beta {:}\\ A\\text{ same, }Z+1;\\quad \\alpha {:}\\ A-4,\\ Z-2","meaning":"nucleon number; nuclear equations","units":""},{"tex":"\\text{mass left }=\\text{ start }\\times (\\tfrac{1}{2})^{n}","meaning":"n = number of half-lives = time ÷ half-life","units":""}],"glossary":[{"term":"Mock exam","def":"A practice exam done under real exam conditions and timing."},{"term":"Item","def":"One multiple-choice question in Paper 01."},{"term":"Stem","def":"The question part of an item."},{"term":"Key","def":"The correct option."},{"term":"Distractor","def":"A wrong option, usually the answer a common mistake gives."},{"term":"Weak topic","def":"A topic where you lost marks; revise these first."},{"term":"Checkpoint","def":"A time by which you should have reached a certain item."},{"term":"Elimination","def":"Crossing out options you know are wrong before choosing."},{"term":"Physics gap","def":"An error caused by not knowing the fact or method."},{"term":"Careless slip","def":"An error made even though you knew the physics (misread units or words)."},{"term":"Alternate candidate","def":"A private candidate who sits Paper 032 instead of submitting SBA marks."}],"studyPlan":[["Day 1 (one sitting)","Sit the full mock: 60 items, STRICTLY 75 minutes, shading the answer grid. No notes, no phone.","75 min",""],["Day 1 (later)","Mark it with your tutor's key. Fill in the scoring table in Unit 16.1","20 min",""],["Day 2","Unit 16.1: weak-topic finder; redo every wrong item; practice 16.1.1–16.1.3","45 min",""],["Day 3","Unit 16.2: copy the revision plan into your diary; practice 16.2.1–16.2.3","30 min",""],["Every day","Formula & Fact Card: cover the left column and test yourself","10 min",""]],"lessons":[{"id":"week16.mock","kind":"mock","paper":"P01","minutes":75,"title":"Full Paper 01 Mock (60 MCQs)","titleText":"Full Paper 01 Mock (60 MCQs)","check":true,"rules":[{"type":"h","text":"Mock Paper 01 — instructions (read before you start)"},{"type":"bullets","items":["**Time allowed: 1 hour 15 minutes (75 minutes).** Write your start time here: ______  finish time: ______","There are **60 items**. Each item has four options (A), (B), (C), (D). Choose the **ONE best** answer.","Shade your answer on the **answer grid** below: shade the box under the letter you choose. Use a pencil so you can rub out cleanly.","If you change your mind, rub out your first mark COMPLETELY. Two shaded boxes on one row scores zero.","Every item is worth 1 mark. **There is no penalty for a wrong answer**, so answer every item.","You may use a silent, non-programmable calculator. Take ⟪g=10\\,\\mathrm{N}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1}⟫ and ⟪c=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ unless the item says otherwise.","The 60 items are in the section **'Exam-Level Questions: Paper 01 Style'** after Unit 16.2 (items M1–M60). Turn there when you are ready.","Pace yourself: about **75 seconds per item**. Checkpoints: item 20 by 0:25, item 40 by 0:50, item 60 by 1:10, leaving 5 minutes to check."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Section","Items","Number of items"],"rows":[["A Mechanics","M1–M16","16"],["B Thermal Physics & Kinetic Theory","M17–M26","10"],["C Waves & Optics","M27–M37","11"],["D Electricity & Magnetism","M38–M53","16"],["E The Physics of the Atom","M54–M60","7"],["**Total**","","**60**"]]},{"type":"warn","text":"Do NOT read Unit 16.1 or 16.2 until you have finished and marked the mock. The tables there tell you the topic of each item.","label":"BEFORE YOU START"},{"type":"h","text":"Answer grid — shade ONE letter per item"},{"type":"table","headers":["Item","A","B","C","D"],"rows":[["1","A","B","C","D"],["2","A","B","C","D"],["3","A","B","C","D"],["4","A","B","C","D"],["5","A","B","C","D"],["6","A","B","C","D"],["7","A","B","C","D"],["8","A","B","C","D"],["9","A","B","C","D"],["10","A","B","C","D"],["11","A","B","C","D"],["12","A","B","C","D"],["13","A","B","C","D"],["14","A","B","C","D"],["15","A","B","C","D"],["16","A","B","C","D"],["17","A","B","C","D"],["18","A","B","C","D"],["19","A","B","C","D"],["20","A","B","C","D"],["21","A","B","C","D"],["22","A","B","C","D"],["23","A","B","C","D"],["24","A","B","C","D"],["25","A","B","C","D"],["26","A","B","C","D"],["27","A","B","C","D"],["28","A","B","C","D"],["29","A","B","C","D"],["30","A","B","C","D"],["31","A","B","C","D"],["32","A","B","C","D"],["33","A","B","C","D"],["34","A","B","C","D"],["35","A","B","C","D"],["36","A","B","C","D"],["37","A","B","C","D"],["38","A","B","C","D"],["39","A","B","C","D"],["40","A","B","C","D"],["41","A","B","C","D"],["42","A","B","C","D"],["43","A","B","C","D"],["44","A","B","C","D"],["45","A","B","C","D"],["46","A","B","C","D"],["47","A","B","C","D"],["48","A","B","C","D"],["49","A","B","C","D"],["50","A","B","C","D"],["51","A","B","C","D"],["52","A","B","C","D"],["53","A","B","C","D"],["54","A","B","C","D"],["55","A","B","C","D"],["56","A","B","C","D"],["57","A","B","C","D"],["58","A","B","C","D"],["59","A","B","C","D"],["60","A","B","C","D"]]},{"type":"fill","text":"**My score: ______ / 60.**  Time I actually took: ______ minutes.","lines":1}],"total":60,"mcq":[{"id":"week16.mock.m1","kind":"mcq","q":"Galileo's most important contribution to the scientific method was that he","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["tested ideas by doing experiments and making careful measurements","showed that heavy objects fall faster than light objects","accepted the ideas of Aristotle without testing them","explained gravity using three laws of motion"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Galileo tested ideas with experiments (inclined planes, pendulums) and measured carefully instead of just arguing. B is Aristotle's idea, which Galileo showed was WRONG (without air resistance, all objects fall with the same acceleration). D describes Newton.","section":"A","topic":"Galileo and the scientific method (A1.1)","revise":"week01"},{"id":"week16.mock.m2","kind":"mcq","q":"A student times 20 complete oscillations of a simple pendulum and gets 36.0 s. The period of the pendulum is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["0.56 s","1.8 s","3.6 s","18 s"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Period = total time ÷ number of oscillations ⟪=36.0\\div 20=1.8\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫. A (0.56 s) is ⟪20\\div 36⟫, the division upside down. D divides by 2 instead of 20.","section":"A","topic":"Simple pendulum, period (A1.2)","revise":"week01"},{"id":"week16.mock.m3","kind":"mcq","q":"A student finds the mass of a stone is 25.4 g and its volume is 9.45 cm^3^. Her calculator shows the density as 2.687830... g cm^−3^. She should record the density as","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["2.6878 g cm^−3^","2.688 g cm^−3^","2.69 g cm^−3^","2.7 g cm^−3^"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Both measurements have 3 significant figures, so the answer is given to 3 s.f.: 2.69 g cm^−3^. B (4 s.f.) claims more precision than the measurements have; D (2 s.f.) throws away precision.","section":"A","topic":"Significant figures (A1.6)","revise":"week01"},{"id":"week16.mock.m4","kind":"mcq","q":"A stone of mass 54 g is lowered into a measuring cylinder. The water level rises from 50 cm^3^ to 70 cm^3^. The density of the stone is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["0.37 g cm^−3^","0.77 g cm^−3^","1.1 g cm^−3^","2.7 g cm^−3^"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Volume of stone ⟪=70-50=20\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫ (displacement method). ⟪\\rho =m\\div V=54\\div 20=2.7\\,\\mathrm{g}\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{-3}⟫. B uses the final reading ⟪(54\\div 70)⟫ instead of the CHANGE in volume; A is the division upside down ⟪(20\\div 54)⟫.","section":"A","topic":"Density of an irregular solid (A1.10)","revise":"week01"},{"id":"week16.mock.m5","kind":"mcq","q":"A boat is steered due north at 4.0 m s^−1^ across a river. The river flows due east at 3.0 m s^−1^. The size of the resultant velocity of the boat is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["1.0 m s^−1^","5.0 m s^−1^","7.0 m s^−1^","12 m s^−1^"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The two velocities are at 90°, so use Pythagoras: ⟪R=\\sqrt{4.0^{2}+3.0^{2}}=\\sqrt{25}=5.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫. C (7.0) adds them as if they were in the same direction; A subtracts them as if they were opposite.","section":"A","topic":"Resultant of perpendicular vectors (A2.3)","revise":"week02"},{"id":"week16.mock.m6","kind":"mcq","q":"The diagram shows a uniform beam balanced at its centre. A 3.0 N weight hangs 40 cm from the pivot. What force F, acting 30 cm from the pivot, keeps the beam balanced?","marks":1,"level":1,"img":{"src":"img/week16/week16_lever","alt":"The diagram shows a uniform beam balanced at its centre. A 3.0 N weight hangs 40 cm from the pivot. What force F, acting 30 cm from the pivot, keeps the beam balanced?","w":1094,"h":381},"options":["2.25 N","3.0 N","4.0 N","12 N"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Principle of moments: clockwise moment = anticlockwise moment. ⟪F\\times 30=3.0\\times 40⟫, so ⟪F=120\\div 30=4.0\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫. A (2.25 N) puts the distances the wrong way round ⟪(3.0\\times 30\\div 40)⟫. B wrongly assumes equal forces balance at unequal distances.","section":"A","topic":"Moments, principle of moments (A3.8–A3.9)","revise":"week02"},{"id":"week16.mock.m7","kind":"mcq","q":"A spring has a natural length of 15 cm. A load of 6.0 N makes its length 18 cm. Assuming Hooke's law is obeyed, what is the LENGTH of the spring when the load is 10 N?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["5.0 cm","20 cm","25 cm","30 cm"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Extension with ⟪6.0\\,\\mathrm{N}=18-15=3.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫, so ⟪k=6.0\\div 3.0=2.0\\,\\mathrm{N}\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{-1}⟫. With 10 N, ⟪x=10\\div 2.0=5.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫, so the length ⟪=15+5.0=20\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫. A is only the EXTENSION. D (30 cm) wrongly makes the total LENGTH proportional to the force.","section":"A","topic":"Hooke's ⟪lawF=kx\\ (A3.14)⟫","revise":"week02"},{"id":"week16.mock.m8","kind":"mcq","q":"The velocity–time graph shows a car travelling at a steady speed and then braking. What is the deceleration of the car while it brakes?","marks":1,"level":1,"img":{"src":"img/week16/week16_vt","alt":"The velocity–time graph shows a car travelling at a steady speed and then braking. What is the deceleration of the car while it brakes?","w":932,"h":574},"options":["0.20 m s^−2^","3.6 m s^−2^","5.0 m s^−2^","12.5 m s^−2^"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Deceleration = change in velocity ÷ time taken ⟪=25\\div (7-2)=25\\div 5=5.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫ (the gradient of the sloping line). B (3.6) divides by the whole 7 s instead of the 5 s of braking. A is the division upside down.","section":"A","topic":"Velocity–time graph: gradient (A4.2)","revise":"week03"},{"id":"week16.mock.m9","kind":"mcq","q":"Using the SAME velocity–time graph, what is the total distance travelled by the car in the 7 s?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["62.5 m","87.5 m","112.5 m","175 m"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Distance = area under the graph = rectangle ⟪(2\\times 25=50\\,\\mathrm{m})⟫ + triangle ⟪(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 5\\times 25=62.5\\,\\mathrm{m})=112.5\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫. A is only the braking distance. B treats the whole graph as one triangle ⟪(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 7\\times 25)⟫. D treats it as one rectangle ⟪(7\\times 25)⟫.","section":"A","topic":"Velocity–time graph: area (A4.2)","revise":"week03"},{"id":"week16.mock.m10","kind":"mcq","q":"A spacecraft is far out in space, far from any planet or star. Its engines are switched off while it moves at 2 km s^−1^. The spacecraft will","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["gradually slow down and stop","speed up steadily","move along a curved path","continue in a straight line at 2 km s^−1^"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Newton's first law: with no resultant force, an object keeps moving at constant velocity (same speed, straight line). A is Aristotle's old idea that a force is needed to KEEP something moving; Newton showed this is wrong.","section":"A","topic":"Newton's first law vs Aristotle (A4.3–A4.4)","revise":"week03"},{"id":"week16.mock.m11","kind":"mcq","q":"A 60 kg skater and a 40 kg skater stand at rest on smooth ice. They push each other apart and the 40 kg skater moves off at 3.0 m s^−1^. The velocity of the 60 kg skater is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["2.0 m s^−1^ in the opposite direction","2.0 m s^−1^ in the same direction","3.0 m s^−1^ in the opposite direction","4.5 m s^−1^ in the opposite direction"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Total momentum before ⟪=0⟫, so after: ⟪60v+40\\times 3.0=0⟫, giving v = −120 ÷ 60 = −2.0 m s^−1^ (2.0 m s^−1^ in the OPPOSITE direction). B has the right size but the wrong direction: the momenta must cancel. D uses ⟪60\\times 3.0\\div 40⟫, the masses the wrong way round.","section":"A","topic":"Conservation of momentum (A4.8)","revise":"week03"},{"id":"week16.mock.m12","kind":"mcq","q":"A ball is thrown vertically upwards at 8.0 m s^−1^. Ignoring air resistance, the maximum height it reaches is ⟪(g=10\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2})⟫","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["3.2 m","6.4 m","32 m","64 m"],"answer":"A","explanation":"KE at the start ⟪=PE⟫ at the top: ⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}mv^{2}=mgh⟫, so ⟪h=v^{2}\\div 2\\mathrm{g}=64\\div 20=3.2\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫. B (6.4 m) forgets the ⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\ (v^{2}\\div g)⟫. D is just v^2^.","section":"A","topic":"Conservation of energy, E~k~ and E~p~ (A5.7, A5.9–A5.10)","revise":"week04"},{"id":"week16.mock.m13","kind":"mcq","q":"In a hydroelectric power station, the main energy changes are","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["chemical → thermal → electrical","electrical → kinetic → gravitational potential","kinetic → gravitational potential → electrical","gravitational potential → kinetic → electrical"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Water stored high in a dam has gravitational PE; it falls and gains KE; the moving water turns turbines and generators, giving electrical energy. C has the first two steps in the wrong order. A describes a fossil-fuel power station.","section":"A","topic":"Energy transformations; alternative sources (A5.3, A5.5)","revise":"week04"},{"id":"week16.mock.m14","kind":"mcq","q":"An electric pump lifts 300 kg of water through a height of 8.0 m in 60 s. The useful power of the pump is ⟪(g=10\\,\\mathrm{N}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1})⟫","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["40 W","400 W","2400 W","24 000 W"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Energy ⟪=mgh=300\\times 10\\times 8.0=24\\,000\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫. Power = energy ÷ time ⟪=24\\,000\\div 60=400\\,\\mathrm{W}⟫. D (24 000) is the ENERGY in joules, not the power. A forgets g.","section":"A","topic":"Power ⟪P=E\\div t\\ (A5.11)⟫","revise":"week04"},{"id":"week16.mock.m15","kind":"mcq","q":"A box of weight 600 N rests on the floor. Its base measures 0.50 m by 0.40 m. The pressure it exerts on the floor is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["120 Pa","1200 Pa","1500 Pa","3000 Pa"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Area ⟪=0.50\\times 0.40=0.20\\,\\mathrm{m}^{2}⟫. ⟪P=F\\div A=600\\div 0.20=3000\\,\\mathrm{Pa}⟫. A multiplies instead of dividing ⟪(600\\times 0.20)⟫. B and C divide by only one side length.","section":"A","topic":"Pressure ⟪P=F\\div A\\ (A6.1)⟫","revise":"week04"},{"id":"week16.mock.m16","kind":"mcq","q":"A block of wood of mass 2.0 kg floats at rest in water. The weight of the water it displaces is ⟪(g=10\\,\\mathrm{N}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1})⟫","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["20 N","10 N","2.0 N","0 N"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A floating object displaces its own weight of fluid (upthrust = weight, and by Archimedes' principle upthrust = weight of fluid displaced). Weight ⟪=mg=2.0\\times 10=20\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫. C (2.0 N) confuses mass (kg) with weight (N).","section":"A","topic":"Archimedes' principle, flotation (A6.3)","revise":"week04"},{"id":"week16.mock.m17","kind":"mcq","q":"The caloric theory of heat could NOT explain","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["why heat flows from a hot object to a cold object","why solids expand when heated","why boring a cannon produced heat for as long as the boring continued","why mixing hot and cold water gives a temperature in between"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Rumford saw that friction from boring cannon gave out heat without limit. If heat were a fluid (caloric) stored in the metal, it would eventually run out. This supported the kinetic theory: heat is energy from work done. A, B and D could all be 'explained' by caloric flowing from place to place.","section":"B","topic":"Caloric vs kinetic theory (B1.1)","revise":"week05"},{"id":"week16.mock.m18","kind":"mcq","q":"A liquid-in-glass thermometer can be made MORE SENSITIVE by using","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["a narrower capillary bore","a wider capillary bore","a thicker glass wall around the bulb","a shorter stem"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A narrower bore makes the same expansion of liquid move a LONGER distance along the tube, so a small temperature change gives a big change in reading. B does the opposite. C makes it slower to respond, not more sensitive.","section":"B","topic":"Thermometer design: sensitivity (B2.3)","revise":"week05"},{"id":"week16.mock.m19","kind":"mcq","q":"A temperature of −73 °C on the kelvin scale is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["200 K","273 K","346 K","400 K"],"answer":"A","explanation":"⟪T/K=\\theta /{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}+273=-73+273=200\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫. C (346 K) ignores the minus sign and adds ⟪73+273⟫.","section":"B","topic":"Kelvin scale (B2.10)","revise":"week06"},{"id":"week16.mock.m20","kind":"mcq","q":"A gas is heated in a sealed container of fixed volume. Its pressure increases because the gas molecules","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["get bigger","increase in number","attract each other more strongly","move faster and hit the walls more often and harder"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Heating gives the molecules more kinetic energy, so they move faster: they hit the walls more often and with more force, so the pressure rises. A is a common mistake: molecules do not change size. B is impossible in a sealed container.","section":"B","topic":"Kinetic explanation of the pressure law (B2.12)","revise":"week06"},{"id":"week16.mock.m21","kind":"mcq","q":"A gas occupies 300 cm^3^ at 27 °C. It is heated to 127 °C at constant pressure. Its new volume is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["225 cm^3^","300 cm^3^","400 cm^3^","1411 cm^3^"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Charles' law with kelvin temperatures: ⟪V_{2}=V_{1}\\times T_{2}\\div T_{1}=300\\times 400\\div 300=400\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫. D (1411) uses ⟪{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}\\ (300\\times 127\\div 27)⟫. ALWAYS change to kelvin. A has the ratio upside down.","section":"B","topic":"Charles' law (B2.11)","revise":"week06"},{"id":"week16.mock.m22","kind":"mcq","q":"How much heat energy is needed to raise the temperature of 0.40 kg of aluminium from 20 °C to 70 °C? (specific heat capacity of aluminium ⟪=900\\,\\mathrm{J}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}^{-1}⟫)","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["25 200 J","18 000 J","7200 J","360 J"],"answer":"B","explanation":"⟪E_{H}=mc\\Delta \\theta =0.40\\times 900\\times (70-20)=0.40\\times 900\\times 50=18\\,000\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫. A uses the final temperature 70 °C instead of the CHANGE Δθ. D leaves out Δθ completely.","section":"B","topic":"⟪E_{H}=mc\\Delta \\theta \\ (B3.2)⟫","revise":"week05"},{"id":"week16.mock.m23","kind":"mcq","q":"The graph shows how the temperature of a hot liquid changes as it cools. During section QR the substance is","marks":1,"level":1,"img":{"src":"img/week16/week16_cooling","alt":"The graph shows how the temperature of a hot liquid changes as it cools. During section QR the substance is","w":934,"h":574},"options":["giving out latent heat while its temperature stays the same","taking in latent heat while it melts","all gas","losing kinetic energy so its molecules slow down"],"answer":"A","explanation":"QR is flat: the liquid is FREEZING (changing to solid at 80 °C). It gives out latent heat as bonds form, so the temperature stays constant. D is wrong because the temperature (and so the average KE) is NOT changing on the flat part. B is melting, which happens when a solid is HEATED.","section":"B","topic":"Cooling curve, latent heat (B3.4)","revise":"week05"},{"id":"week16.mock.m24","kind":"mcq","q":"A 2.0 kW kettle keeps water boiling. In 115 s the mass of water falls by 0.10 kg. The specific latent heat of vaporisation of water from these results is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["⟪2.3\\times 10^{3}\\,\\mathrm{J}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1}⟫","⟪2.3\\times 10^{4}\\,\\mathrm{J}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1}⟫","⟪2.3\\times 10^{5}\\,\\mathrm{J}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1}⟫","⟪2.3\\times 10^{6}\\,\\mathrm{J}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1}⟫"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Energy ⟪=Pt=2000\\times 115=230\\,000\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫. ⟪l=E\\div m=230\\,000\\div 0.10=2.3\\times 10^{6}\\,\\mathrm{J}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1}⟫. A uses 2.0 W instead of 2000 W (forgetting that ⟪k=1000⟫).","section":"B","topic":"Specific latent heat E~H~ = ml (B3.5–B3.6)","revise":"week05"},{"id":"week16.mock.m25","kind":"mcq","q":"Sweat on your skin cools you as it evaporates because","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["the slowest molecules escape from the liquid","the liquid takes in 'cold' from the air","all the molecules slow down by the same amount","the most energetic molecules escape, lowering the average kinetic energy of those left"],"answer":"D","explanation":"In evaporation the FASTEST (most energetic) molecules escape from the surface. The average KE of the remaining liquid falls, so its temperature falls and it takes heat from your skin. A is the reverse. B is wrong: 'cold' is not a thing that flows; heat flows.","section":"B","topic":"Evaporation and cooling (B3.7)","revise":"week05"},{"id":"week16.mock.m26","kind":"mcq","q":"An air-conditioning unit is fitted high up on the wall of a room because","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["cold air rises to the ceiling","cool air is denser and sinks, setting up a convection current","cool air is a good conductor of heat","radiation travels downwards"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The cool air from the unit is denser, so it sinks; warm air rises to replace it and is cooled. This convection current circulates through the whole room. A is wrong: it is WARM air that rises. C is wrong: air is a poor conductor.","section":"B","topic":"Convection and its applications (B4.2, B4.6)","revise":"week06"},{"id":"week16.mock.m27","kind":"mcq","q":"In a longitudinal wave, the particles of the medium","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["vibrate at right angles to the direction the wave travels","move in circles","do not vibrate at all","vibrate back and forth along the direction the wave travels"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Longitudinal waves (e.g. sound) have vibrations PARALLEL to the direction of travel, forming compressions and rarefactions. A describes a TRANSVERSE wave (e.g. light, water waves).","section":"C","topic":"Transverse and longitudinal waves (C1.1)","revise":"week07"},{"id":"week16.mock.m28","kind":"mcq","q":"The graph shows how the displacement of one particle in a wave changes with time. The frequency of the wave is","marks":1,"level":1,"img":{"src":"img/week16/week16_wave","alt":"The graph shows how the displacement of one particle in a wave changes with time. The frequency of the wave is","w":1054,"h":534},"options":["0.25 Hz","125 Hz","250 Hz","500 Hz"],"answer":"C","explanation":"One complete cycle takes 4 ms, so ⟪T=0.004\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫ and ⟪f=1\\div T=1\\div 0.004=250\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫. A forgets to change ms to ⟪s\\ (1\\div 4)⟫. B uses the whole 8 ms shown (two cycles) as one period. D uses half a cycle (2 ms).","section":"C","topic":"Displacement–time graphs, f = 1 ÷ T (C1.2–C1.3)","revise":"week07"},{"id":"week16.mock.m29","kind":"mcq","q":"A radio station broadcasts at a frequency of 100 MHz. The wavelength of its waves is ⟪(c=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1})⟫","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["0.33 m","3.0 m","30 m","⟪3.0\\times 10^{16}\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"],"answer":"B","explanation":"⟪\\lambda =v\\div f=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\div (100\\times 10^{6})=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫. A uses ⟪f\\div c⟫, the formula upside down ⟪(10^{8}\\div 3.0\\times 10^{8})⟫. D multiplies instead of dividing.","section":"C","topic":"Wave equation v = fλ (C1.2, C3.1)","revise":"week07"},{"id":"week16.mock.m30","kind":"mcq","q":"During a storm, thunder is heard 3.0 s after the lightning is seen. The speed of sound in air is 340 m s^−1^. How far away is the storm?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["113 m","510 m","1020 m","2040 m"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Light arrives almost instantly, so the sound takes 3.0 s to travel one way: ⟪d=v\\times t=340\\times 3.0=1020\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫. B halves the distance as if it were an ECHO (there and back). A divides instead of multiplying.","section":"C","topic":"Speed of sound in practical situations (C2.3)","revise":"week07"},{"id":"week16.mock.m31","kind":"mcq","q":"A musical note is played more loudly but at the same pitch. Compared with the first sound wave, the new wave has","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["a larger amplitude and the same frequency","a larger frequency and the same amplitude","a larger amplitude and a larger frequency","a smaller wavelength and the same amplitude"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Loudness depends on amplitude; pitch depends on frequency. Louder → bigger amplitude. Same pitch → same frequency. B mixes the two up: a higher frequency would give a HIGHER pitch.","section":"C","topic":"Pitch and loudness (C2.2)","revise":"week07"},{"id":"week16.mock.m32","kind":"mcq","q":"You can hear people talking in the next room through an open doorway, although you cannot see them. This is mainly because","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["sound travels faster than light","sound waves are longitudinal but light waves are transverse","light cannot pass through a doorway","sound waves have a wavelength similar to the width of the doorway, so they diffract around it"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Diffraction is greatest when the gap is about the same size as the wavelength. Sound wavelengths (about 0.3–3 m) are similar to a doorway, so sound spreads around it. Light's wavelength (about ⟪5\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫) is far too small to diffract noticeably. B is true but is not the reason. A is false.","section":"C","topic":"Diffraction of sound and light (C2.4, C4.3)","revise":"week07"},{"id":"week16.mock.m33","kind":"mcq","q":"A television remote control sends signals to the television using","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["radio waves","microwaves","infrared radiation","ultraviolet radiation"],"answer":"C","explanation":"TV remote controls use infrared (an LED gives out infrared pulses). Radio waves (A) are used for broadcasting over long distances, not for the remote control handset.","section":"C","topic":"EM spectrum: uses (C3.3)","revise":"week07"},{"id":"week16.mock.m34","kind":"mcq","q":"A total eclipse of the Sun is seen when","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["the Earth is between the Sun and the Moon","the Moon is between the Sun and the Earth","the Sun is between the Earth and the Moon","the Moon passes into the shadow of the Earth"],"answer":"B","explanation":"In a solar eclipse the Moon blocks the Sun's light and casts a shadow on the Earth. This works because light travels in straight lines. A and D describe an eclipse of the MOON (lunar eclipse).","section":"C","topic":"Rectilinear propagation: eclipses (C4.4)","revise":"week08"},{"id":"week16.mock.m35","kind":"mcq","q":"A girl stands 2.0 m in front of a plane mirror. She walks 0.50 m towards the mirror. The distance between the girl and her image is now","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["3.0 m","2.5 m","1.5 m","1.0 m"],"answer":"A","explanation":"She is ⟪now2.0-0.50=1.5\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ from the mirror. The image is as far behind the mirror as she is in front, so the girl-to-image distance ⟪=1.5+1.5=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫. C (1.5 m) is only her distance to the MIRROR.","section":"C","topic":"Plane-mirror images (C4.6)","revise":"week08"},{"id":"week16.mock.m36","kind":"mcq","q":"A ray of light travels through a semicircular glass block (refractive index 1.5) towards the centre of its flat face, as shown. The angle of incidence at the flat face is 48°. What happens to the ray at the flat face?","marks":1,"level":1,"img":{"src":"img/week16/week16_tir","alt":"A ray of light travels through a semicircular glass block (refractive index 1.5) towards the centre of its flat face, as shown. The angle of incidence at the flat face is 48°. What happens to the ray at the flat face?","w":675,"h":574},"options":["It is refracted away from the normal into the air","It is totally internally reflected","It is refracted towards the normal","It passes straight through without bending"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Critical angle: ⟪\\sin c=1\\div n=1\\div 1.5=0.667⟫, so ⟪c\\approx 41.8^{\\circ}⟫. The angle of incidence (48°) is GREATER than c, so the light is totally internally reflected. A would happen only if the angle were LESS than 41.8°.","section":"C","topic":"Critical angle and total internal reflection (C4.11–C4.12)","revise":"week08"},{"id":"week16.mock.m37","kind":"mcq","q":"An object is placed 6 cm from a converging lens of focal length 10 cm, as shown. The image formed is","marks":1,"level":1,"img":{"src":"img/week16/week16_lens","alt":"An object is placed 6 cm from a converging lens of focal length 10 cm, as shown. The image formed is","w":1174,"h":494},"options":["real, inverted and magnified","real, inverted and diminished","virtual, upright and diminished","virtual, upright and magnified"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The object is INSIDE the focal length (6 cm < 10 cm), so the lens acts as a magnifying glass: the image is virtual, upright and magnified, on the same side as the object. A happens when the object is between F and 2F.","section":"C","topic":"Converging lens images (C5.3)","revise":"week08"},{"id":"week16.mock.m38","kind":"mcq","q":"A polythene rod becomes negatively charged when it is rubbed with a woollen cloth because","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["electrons move from the cloth to the rod","protons move from the rod to the cloth","electrons move from the rod to the cloth","protons move from the cloth to the rod"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Only electrons move when objects are rubbed (protons are held in the nucleus). The rod GAINS electrons, so it becomes negative. C is the reverse; it would make the rod positive. B and D are impossible because protons do not move.","section":"D","topic":"Charging by friction (D1.1)","revise":"es01"},{"id":"week16.mock.m39","kind":"mcq","q":"Before an aircraft is refuelled, it is connected to the ground by a conducting cable. This is done to","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["hold the aircraft still","increase the flow of fuel","charge the fuel so it burns better","let charge flow away to earth so that no spark ignites the fuel"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Fuel rubbing in the pipe can build up static charge. The earthing cable lets the charge flow safely to earth, so no spark can jump and ignite the fuel vapour. C is wrong: charge on the fuel is the HAZARD, not a benefit.","section":"D","topic":"Hazards of static electricity (D1.5)","revise":"es01"},{"id":"week16.mock.m40","kind":"mcq","q":"A charge of 1800 C passes through a heater in 5.0 minutes. The current in the heater is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["0.17 A","6.0 A","360 A","9000 A"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Q = It, so ⟪I=Q\\div t=1800\\div (5.0\\times 60)=1800\\div 300=6.0\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫. C (360 A) forgets to change minutes into seconds. D multiplies instead of dividing.","section":"D","topic":"Charge Q = It (D2.5)","revise":"week09"},{"id":"week16.mock.m41","kind":"mcq","q":"Which voltage–time graph shows the output from an AC supply after HALF-WAVE rectification by a single diode?","marks":1,"level":1,"img":{"src":"img/week16/week16_vgraphs","alt":"Which voltage–time graph shows the output from an AC supply after HALF-WAVE rectification by a single diode?","w":1400,"h":351},"options":["Graph A","Graph B","Graph C","Graph D"],"answer":"C","explanation":"A diode lets current pass in ONE direction only, so the negative half of each cycle is removed: positive humps with flat gaps (Graph C). A is steady DC from a battery; B is unrectified AC; D is full-wave rectification (needs four diodes).","section":"D","topic":"AC, DC and half-wave rectification (D2.7, D5.1–D5.2)","revise":"week10"},{"id":"week16.mock.m42","kind":"mcq","q":"In the circuit shown, the battery has negligible internal resistance. What is the reading on the ammeter?","marks":1,"level":1,"img":{"src":"img/week16/week16_circuit","alt":"In the circuit shown, the battery has negligible internal resistance. What is the reading on the ammeter?","w":853,"h":574},"options":["5.0 A","2.0 A","1.1 A","0.83 A"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Parallel part: ⟪1\\div R=1\\div 6+1\\div 3=3\\div 6⟫, so ⟪R=2\\,\\Omega⟫. Total resistance ⟪=3+2=5\\,\\Omega⟫. ⟪I=V\\div R=10\\div 5=2.0\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫. D (0.83 A) adds all three resistors in series (12 Ω). A uses only the 2 Ω parallel part.","section":"D","topic":"Series–parallel circuits (D4.11–D4.12)","revise":"week09"},{"id":"week16.mock.m43","kind":"mcq","q":"In the SAME circuit, what is the potential difference across the 6 Ω resistor?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["4.0 V","6.0 V","10 V","12 V"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The parallel section has a resistance of 2 Ω and carries the full 2.0 A, so ⟪V=IR=2.0\\times 2=4.0\\,\\mathrm{V}⟫. Both parallel resistors have this same p.d. D (12 V) wrongly uses ⟪V=2.0\\,\\mathrm{A}\\times 6\\,\\Omega⟫, but only part of the current goes through the 6 Ω resistor.","section":"D","topic":"Series–parallel circuits, V = IR (D4.8, D4.12)","revise":"week09"},{"id":"week16.mock.m44","kind":"mcq","q":"A 1.5 kW air conditioner runs for 6.0 hours. The electrical energy it uses is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["0.25 kWh","4.0 kWh","9.0 kWh","9000 kWh"],"answer":"C","explanation":"⟪E=P\\times t=1.5\\,\\mathrm{kW}\\times 6.0\\,\\mathrm{h}=9.0\\,\\mathrm{kWh}⟫. D uses 1500 W instead of 1.5 kW: kWh needs power in KILOWATTS and time in HOURS. A divides instead of multiplying.","section":"D","topic":"Electrical energy in kWh (D3.3)","revise":"week09"},{"id":"week16.mock.m45","kind":"mcq","q":"A thermistor is used in a fire-alarm circuit because its resistance","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["increases greatly as its temperature rises","decreases greatly as its temperature rises","stays constant at all temperatures","changes when light falls on it"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A (negative temperature coefficient) thermistor's resistance FALLS sharply as it gets hotter, so the current rises and can trigger the alarm. A describes a filament lamp (a small rise). D describes an LDR.","section":"D","topic":"Thermistor characteristics (D4.6)","revise":"week09"},{"id":"week16.mock.m46","kind":"mcq","q":"Electricity is transmitted across the country at a very high voltage because","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["electrons travel faster at a high voltage","a high voltage produces a larger current in the cables","for the same power, the current is smaller, so less energy is wasted as heat in the cables","high voltages are safer for people living near the cables"],"answer":"C","explanation":"⟪P=IV⟫, so for the same power a HIGH voltage means a SMALL current. Heat loss in the cables depends on I^2^R, so a small current wastes much less energy. B is the opposite of what happens. D is false: high voltage is very dangerous.","section":"D","topic":"Advantages of AC for transmission (D7.14)","revise":"week11"},{"id":"week16.mock.m47","kind":"mcq","q":"The appliances in a house are connected in parallel so that","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["the same current flows through each appliance","the total resistance of the circuit is larger","one switch controls all the appliances","each appliance gets the full mains voltage and can be switched on and off separately"],"answer":"D","explanation":"In parallel, every branch is connected across the supply, so each appliance gets the full mains voltage and has its own switch. A describes a SERIES circuit (same current everywhere). B is wrong: adding parallel branches DECREASES the total resistance.","section":"D","topic":"Parallel wiring in homes (D4.13)","revise":"week10"},{"id":"week16.mock.m48","kind":"mcq","q":"The fuse in a plug is connected in the LIVE wire so that, when the fuse melts,","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["the appliance is disconnected from the high voltage of the supply","the current flows to earth through the neutral wire","the voltage of the supply increases","the appliance keeps working at a lower current"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The live wire carries the high voltage. A fuse in the live wire cuts the appliance off from that voltage, so it is safe to touch. If the fuse were in the neutral wire, the appliance would still be 'live' after the fuse blew. B is wrong: the earth wire, not the neutral, carries fault current to earth.","section":"D","topic":"Fuses and safety (D4.14)","revise":"week10"},{"id":"week16.mock.m49","kind":"mcq","q":"The logic circuit shown is made from a NOT gate and a NAND gate. Which inputs give an output ⟪Q=0⟫?","marks":1,"level":1,"img":{"src":"img/week16/week16_logic","alt":"The logic circuit shown is made from a NOT gate and a NAND gate. Which inputs give an output Q = 0?","w":1094,"h":416},"options":["⟪A=0⟫, ⟪B=0⟫","⟪A=0⟫, ⟪B=1⟫","⟪A=1⟫, ⟪B=0⟫","⟪A=1⟫, ⟪B=1⟫"],"answer":"B","explanation":"X = NOT A. A NAND gate gives 0 ONLY when both of its inputs are 1, so we need ⟪X=1⟫ and ⟪B=1⟫, i.e. ⟪A=0⟫ and ⟪B=1⟫. D (A = 1, ⟪B=1⟫) forgets the NOT gate: then ⟪X=0⟫, so ⟪Q=1⟫.","section":"D","topic":"Logic gate combinations and truth tables (D5.3–D5.5)","revise":"week10"},{"id":"week16.mock.m50","kind":"mcq","q":"Steel, rather than soft iron, is used to make a permanent magnet because steel","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["is easily magnetised and easily demagnetised","is a non-magnetic material","keeps its magnetism once it has been magnetised","is a better conductor of electricity"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Steel is harder to magnetise but KEEPS its magnetism, so it makes a good permanent magnet. A describes soft iron, which is used for temporary magnets (electromagnet cores, relays).","section":"D","topic":"Permanent vs temporary magnets (D6.3)","revise":"week10"},{"id":"week16.mock.m51","kind":"mcq","q":"In a simple DC motor, the split-ring commutator","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["reverses the direction of the current in the coil every half turn","increases the size of the current in the coil","reverses the direction of the magnetic field every half turn","changes AC into DC"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Every half turn, the commutator reverses the current in the coil, so the forces on the sides of the coil keep turning it the SAME way. C is wrong: the magnets' field does not change. D describes a rectifier.","section":"D","topic":"The DC motor (D7.8)","revise":"week10"},{"id":"week16.mock.m52","kind":"mcq","q":"A bar magnet is held STILL inside a coil that is connected to a sensitive galvanometer. The galvanometer shows","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["a steady deflection","a deflection that increases with time","a deflection that changes direction again and again","no deflection"],"answer":"D","explanation":"An emf is induced only when the magnetic field through the coil CHANGES (the magnet or coil must move). With the magnet still there is no change in flux, so there is no induced current. A is the common mistake: just having a magnet in the coil does not give a current.","section":"D","topic":"Electromagnetic induction (D7.9–D7.10)","revise":"week11"},{"id":"week16.mock.m53","kind":"mcq","q":"An ideal transformer has 200 turns on its primary coil, which is connected to a 12 V AC supply. The output voltage is 240 V. The number of turns on the secondary coil is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["10","240","2400","4000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"⟪V_{p}\\div V_{s}=N_{p}\\div N_{s}⟫, so ⟪N_{s}=N_{p}\\times V_{s}\\div V_{p}=200\\times 240\\div 12=4000⟫ turns. A (10) puts the ratio upside down ⟪(200\\times 12\\div 240)⟫. A step-UP transformer must have MORE turns on the secondary.","section":"D","topic":"Ideal transformer equation (D7.15)","revise":"week11"},{"id":"week16.mock.m54","kind":"mcq","q":"Which scientist discovered the neutron?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["Bohr","Chadwick","Rutherford","Thomson"],"answer":"B","explanation":"James Chadwick discovered the neutron in 1932. Rutherford (C) discovered the nucleus (and named the proton); Thomson discovered the electron; Bohr put electrons in fixed shells.","section":"E","topic":"Development of the atomic model (E1.1)","revise":"week12"},{"id":"week16.mock.m55","kind":"mcq","q":"In the Geiger–Marsden experiment, a very small number of alpha particles bounced back from the gold foil. This showed that","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["the atom is mostly empty space","electrons move in fixed shells","the positive charge and most of the mass of an atom are in a tiny nucleus","atoms cannot be divided"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Only a tiny, dense, positive nucleus could repel the positive alpha particles strongly enough to send some straight back. A is true, but it comes from the fact that MOST particles went straight through, not from the few that bounced back.","section":"E","topic":"Geiger–Marsden experiment (E1.2)","revise":"week12"},{"id":"week16.mock.m56","kind":"mcq","q":"A neutral atom of sodium has nucleon number 23 and proton number 11. Which row gives the numbers of particles in this atom?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["11 protons, 12 neutrons, 11 electrons","11 protons, 23 neutrons, 11 electrons","12 protons, 11 neutrons, 12 electrons","23 protons, 11 neutrons, 23 electrons"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Protons ⟪=Z=11⟫. Neutrons ⟪=A-Z=23-11=12⟫. A neutral atom has electrons = protons ⟪=11⟫. B uses the nucleon number (23) as the number of neutrons.","section":"E","topic":"⟪A=Z+N⟫; neutral atoms (E2.3–E2.4)","revise":"week12"},{"id":"week16.mock.m57","kind":"mcq","q":"A lead nucleus (nucleon number 214, proton number 82) emits a beta particle. The new nucleus has","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["nucleon number 210, proton number 80","nucleon number 214, proton number 81","nucleon number 214, proton number 83","nucleon number 213, proton number 82"],"answer":"C","explanation":"In beta decay a neutron changes into a proton and an electron (emitted). The nucleon number stays the same (214) and the proton number goes UP by 1 (83). B lowers Z instead of raising it. A is the result of ALPHA decay (⟪A-4⟫, ⟪Z-2⟫).","section":"E","topic":"Nuclear equations: beta decay (E3.6)","revise":"week12"},{"id":"week16.mock.m58","kind":"mcq","q":"A radiation passes through a sheet of paper but is stopped by a few millimetres of aluminium. The radiation is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["alpha","beta","gamma","a mixture of alpha and gamma"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Alpha is stopped by paper; beta passes through paper but is stopped by a few mm of aluminium; gamma needs thick lead or concrete. C is wrong because gamma would pass through the aluminium.","section":"E","topic":"Penetrating power of α, β, γ (E3.2–E3.3)","revise":"week12"},{"id":"week16.mock.m59","kind":"mcq","q":"Iodine-131 has a half-life of 8 days. A hospital receives 32 g of iodine-131. What mass of iodine-131 is left after 24 days?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["4.0 g","8.0 g","10.7 g","16 g"],"answer":"A","explanation":"⟪24\\,\\mathrm{days}\\div 8\\,\\mathrm{days}=3⟫ half-lives. 32 → 16 → 8 → 4.0 g. B stops after two half-lives. C divides 32 by 3 instead of halving three times.","section":"E","topic":"Half-life calculations (E3.10)","revise":"week12"},{"id":"week16.mock.m60","kind":"mcq","q":"In a paper mill, a radioactive source and a detector are placed on opposite sides of the paper to check its thickness. The best source to use gives out","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["alpha, because it is stopped by paper","gamma, because it is the most penetrating","alpha, because it is the most ionising","beta, because the amount passing through changes noticeably with the paper's thickness"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Beta particles are partly absorbed by paper, so a small change in thickness gives a measurable change in the detector reading. Gamma (B) would pass straight through whatever the thickness, so the reading would hardly change. Alpha would be stopped completely.","section":"E","topic":"Uses of radioisotopes (E3.11)","revise":"week12"}],"n":121},{"id":"week16.16.1","kind":"unit","unit":"16.1","title":"After the mock: find your weak topics","titleText":"After the mock: find your weak topics","goals":[{"code":"A","text":"Section A Mechanics (16 items): measurement, vectors, moments, Hooke's law, motion graphs, Newton, momentum, energy, power, pressure, flotation"},{"code":"B","text":"Section B Thermal physics (10 items): theories of heat, thermometers, kelvin, gas laws, specific and latent heat, evaporation, convection"},{"code":"C","text":"Section C Waves and optics (11 items): wave types and graphs, ⟪v=f\\lambda⟫, sound, EM uses, eclipses, mirrors, TIR, lenses"},{"code":"D","text":"Section D Electricity and magnetism (16 items): static, Q = It, rectification, circuits, kWh, mains safety, logic, magnets, motor, induction, transformers"},{"code":"E","text":"Section E The atom (7 items): atomic models, Geiger–Marsden, atomic structure, beta decay, penetration, half-life, uses"}],"codes":["A","B","C","D","E"],"words":[{"term":"Item","def":"One multiple-choice question in Paper 01."},{"term":"Stem","def":"The question part of an item."},{"term":"Key","def":"The correct option."},{"term":"Checkpoint","def":"A time by which you should have reached a certain item."},{"term":"Elimination","def":"Crossing out options you know are wrong before choosing."},{"term":"Physics gap","def":"An error caused by not knowing the fact or method."},{"term":"Careless slip","def":"An error made even though you knew the physics (misread units or words)."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"A mock is only useful if you **learn from your mistakes**. A score on its own tells you nothing. The two tables below turn every wrong answer into a topic and a page to revise."},{"type":"steps","items":["Mark your answer grid with the key from your tutor. Put a ✗ next to every wrong item.","Count your score for each section and write it in the **scoring table**. Work out the percentage: score ÷ out of × 100.","Look up each wrong item in the **item-by-item table**: it tells you the topic and which workbook to open.","For each wrong item, decide the **type of error** (see the error table). Write it in your Mistake Log.","Revise your **lowest-percentage section first**, then the next lowest. Redo the wrong items WITHOUT looking at the explanation."]},{"type":"h","text":"Scoring table — one row per section"},{"type":"table","headers":["Section","Items","Out of","My score","My %","What to revise (workbook: items)"],"rows":[["**A Mechanics**","M1–M16","16","","","Week 1 (Measurement): M1, M2, M3, M4; Week 2 (Vectors, Forces, Moments): M5, M6, M7; Week 3 (Motion & Newton): M8, M9, M10, M11; Week 4 (Energy, Power, Pressure): M12, M13, M14, M15, M16"],["**B Thermal Physics**","M17–M26","10","","","Week 5 (Heat & Thermal Measurements): M17, M18, M22, M23, M24, M25; Week 6 (Gas Laws & Heat Transfer): M19, M20, M21, M26"],["**C Waves & Optics**","M27–M37","11","","","Week 7 (Waves, Sound, EM): M27, M28, M29, M30, M31, M32, M33; Week 8 (Light & Lenses): M34, M35, M36, M37"],["**D Electricity & Magnetism**","M38–M53","16","","","1-to-1 S1 (Electrostatics): M38, M39; 1-to-1 S2 (Current & Circuits): M40, M42, M43, M44, M45; 1-to-1 S3 (Mains, Electronics, Magnetism, Motor): M41, M47, M48, M49, M50, M51; 1-to-1 S4 (Induction & Transformers): M46, M52, M53"],["**E The Atom**","M54–M60","7","","","1-to-1 S5 (The Atom): M54, M55, M56, M57, M58, M59, M60"],["**Total**","M1–M60","60","","","Overall: under 30 = urgent revision; 30–44 = keep going; 45+ = strong"]]},{"type":"tip","text":"Any section **below 60%** is a priority. Section A and Section D have 16 items each, so they are worth the most marks: a weak A or D costs you more than a weak E. Use the Week 15 Paper 01 Drill rapid-revision table for the same section as a quick refresher.","label":"WHICH SECTION FIRST?"},{"type":"h","text":"Item-by-item table — what each item tested"},{"type":"table","headers":["Item","Section","Topic (syllabus objective)","Revise in"],"rows":[["M1","A","Galileo and the scientific method (A1.1)","Week 1 (Measurement)"],["M2","A","Simple pendulum, period (A1.2)","Week 1 (Measurement)"],["M3","A","Significant figures (A1.6)","Week 1 (Measurement)"],["M4","A","Density of an irregular solid (A1.10)","Week 1 (Measurement)"],["M5","A","Resultant of perpendicular vectors (A2.3)","Week 2 (Vectors, Forces, Moments)"],["M6","A","Moments, principle of moments (A3.8–A3.9)","Week 2 (Vectors, Forces, Moments)"],["M7","A","Hooke's ⟪lawF=kx\\ (A3.14)⟫","Week 2 (Vectors, Forces, Moments)"],["M8","A","Velocity–time graph: gradient (A4.2)","Week 3 (Motion & Newton)"],["M9","A","Velocity–time graph: area (A4.2)","Week 3 (Motion & Newton)"],["M10","A","Newton's first law vs Aristotle (A4.3–A4.4)","Week 3 (Motion & Newton)"],["M11","A","Conservation of momentum (A4.8)","Week 3 (Motion & Newton)"],["M12","A","Conservation of energy, E~k~ and E~p~ (A5.7, A5.9–A5.10)","Week 4 (Energy, Power, Pressure)"],["M13","A","Energy transformations; alternative sources (A5.3, A5.5)","Week 4 (Energy, Power, Pressure)"],["M14","A","Power ⟪P=E\\div t\\ (A5.11)⟫","Week 4 (Energy, Power, Pressure)"],["M15","A","Pressure ⟪P=F\\div A\\ (A6.1)⟫","Week 4 (Energy, Power, Pressure)"],["M16","A","Archimedes' principle, flotation (A6.3)","Week 4 (Energy, Power, Pressure)"],["M17","B","Caloric vs kinetic theory (B1.1)","Week 5 (Heat & Thermal Measurements)"],["M18","B","Thermometer design: sensitivity (B2.3)","Week 5 (Heat & Thermal Measurements)"],["M19","B","Kelvin scale (B2.10)","Week 6 (Gas Laws & Heat Transfer)"],["M20","B","Kinetic explanation of the pressure law (B2.12)","Week 6 (Gas Laws & Heat Transfer)"],["M21","B","Charles' law (B2.11)","Week 6 (Gas Laws & Heat Transfer)"],["M22","B","⟪E_{H}=mc\\Delta \\theta \\ (B3.2)⟫","Week 5 (Heat & Thermal Measurements)"],["M23","B","Cooling curve, latent heat (B3.4)","Week 5 (Heat & Thermal Measurements)"],["M24","B","Specific latent heat E~H~ = ml (B3.5–B3.6)","Week 5 (Heat & Thermal Measurements)"],["M25","B","Evaporation and cooling (B3.7)","Week 5 (Heat & Thermal Measurements)"],["M26","B","Convection and its applications (B4.2, B4.6)","Week 6 (Gas Laws & Heat Transfer)"],["M27","C","Transverse and longitudinal waves (C1.1)","Week 7 (Waves, Sound, EM)"],["M28","C","Displacement–time graphs, f = 1 ÷ T (C1.2–C1.3)","Week 7 (Waves, Sound, EM)"],["M29","C","Wave equation v = fλ (C1.2, C3.1)","Week 7 (Waves, Sound, EM)"],["M30","C","Speed of sound in practical situations (C2.3)","Week 7 (Waves, Sound, EM)"],["M31","C","Pitch and loudness (C2.2)","Week 7 (Waves, Sound, EM)"],["M32","C","Diffraction of sound and light (C2.4, C4.3)","Week 7 (Waves, Sound, EM)"],["M33","C","EM spectrum: uses (C3.3)","Week 7 (Waves, Sound, EM)"],["M34","C","Rectilinear propagation: eclipses (C4.4)","Week 8 (Light & Lenses)"],["M35","C","Plane-mirror images (C4.6)","Week 8 (Light & Lenses)"],["M36","C","Critical angle and total internal reflection (C4.11–C4.12)","Week 8 (Light & Lenses)"],["M37","C","Converging lens images (C5.3)","Week 8 (Light & Lenses)"],["M38","D","Charging by friction (D1.1)","1-to-1 S1 (Electrostatics)"],["M39","D","Hazards of static electricity (D1.5)","1-to-1 S1 (Electrostatics)"],["M40","D","Charge Q = It (D2.5)","1-to-1 S2 (Current & Circuits)"],["M41","D","AC, DC and half-wave rectification (D2.7, D5.1–D5.2)","1-to-1 S3 (Mains, Electronics, Magnetism, Motor)"],["M42","D","Series–parallel circuits (D4.11–D4.12)","1-to-1 S2 (Current & Circuits)"],["M43","D","Series–parallel circuits, V = IR (D4.8, D4.12)","1-to-1 S2 (Current & Circuits)"],["M44","D","Electrical energy in kWh (D3.3)","1-to-1 S2 (Current & Circuits)"],["M45","D","Thermistor characteristics (D4.6)","1-to-1 S2 (Current & Circuits)"],["M46","D","Advantages of AC for transmission (D7.14)","1-to-1 S4 (Induction & Transformers)"],["M47","D","Parallel wiring in homes (D4.13)","1-to-1 S3 (Mains, Electronics, Magnetism, Motor)"],["M48","D","Fuses and safety (D4.14)","1-to-1 S3 (Mains, Electronics, Magnetism, Motor)"],["M49","D","Logic gate combinations and truth tables (D5.3–D5.5)","1-to-1 S3 (Mains, Electronics, Magnetism, Motor)"],["M50","D","Permanent vs temporary magnets (D6.3)","1-to-1 S3 (Mains, Electronics, Magnetism, Motor)"],["M51","D","The DC motor (D7.8)","1-to-1 S3 (Mains, Electronics, Magnetism, Motor)"],["M52","D","Electromagnetic induction (D7.9–D7.10)","1-to-1 S4 (Induction & Transformers)"],["M53","D","Ideal transformer equation (D7.15)","1-to-1 S4 (Induction & Transformers)"],["M54","E","Development of the atomic model (E1.1)","1-to-1 S5 (The Atom)"],["M55","E","Geiger–Marsden experiment (E1.2)","1-to-1 S5 (The Atom)"],["M56","E","⟪A=Z+N⟫; neutral atoms (E2.3–E2.4)","1-to-1 S5 (The Atom)"],["M57","E","Nuclear equations: beta decay (E3.6)","1-to-1 S5 (The Atom)"],["M58","E","Penetrating power of α, β, γ (E3.2–E3.3)","1-to-1 S5 (The Atom)"],["M59","E","Half-life calculations (E3.10)","1-to-1 S5 (The Atom)"],["M60","E","Uses of radioisotopes (E3.11)","1-to-1 S5 (The Atom)"]]},{"type":"h","text":"What kind of mistake was it?"},{"type":"table","headers":["Error type","How you know","What to do"],"rows":[["**Physics gap**","You did not know the fact or formula, or could not start","Re-read the notes in the matching workbook; learn the Formula & Fact Card; redo its Exam-Level MCQs"],["**Careless slip**","You knew it but misread the item, e.g. minutes vs seconds, °C vs K, missed NOT","Underline key words and units in every stem; check powers of ten"],["**Technique**","You ran out of time or guessed between two options","Use elimination; skip long items and come back; keep to the checkpoints"]]},{"type":"remember","text":"A 'careless slip' is still a lost mark. If most of your errors are slips, the fix is technique, not more notes."}],"examples":[{"id":"week16.16.1.e1","title":"Turning wrong answers into a revision list","question":"Kevon got these items wrong: M8, M9, M21, M42 and M43. What should he revise?","steps":[{"label":"Look up","text":"M8 and M9: velocity–time graph (gradient and area), Week 3. M21: Charles' law, Week 6. M42 and M43: series–parallel circuit, 1-to-1 Session 2."},{"label":"Spot the pattern","text":"Two graph items and two circuit items on the SAME diagram: one topic gap cost him two marks each time."},{"label":"Plan","text":"Week 3 Unit on motion graphs (gradient = acceleration, area = distance); Session 2 Unit on series–parallel circuits; Week 6 gas-law calculations (kelvin!)."}],"answer":"Revise v–t graphs (Week 3), series–parallel circuits (Session 2) and Charles' law with kelvin (Week 6), then redo M8, M9, M21, M42, M43."}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week16.16.1.p1","kind":"self","q":"Andre's mock scores were: A 11/16, B 8/10, C 6/11, D 13/16, E 4/7. Work out his percentage for each section. Which section should he revise first, and which workbooks should he open?","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":5,"scheme":"A 69%, B 80%, C 55%, D 81%, E 57% (1). Lowest is Section C (55%), so revise it first (1): Week 7 (Waves, Sound, EM) and Week 8 (Light and Lenses); then Section E, 1-to-1 Session 5 (1).","points":[{"text":"A 69%, B 80%, C 55%, D 81%, E 57%","marks":1},{"text":"Lowest is Section C (55%), so revise it first","marks":1},{"text":": Week 7 (Waves, Sound, EM) and Week 8 (Light and Lenses); then Section E, 1-to-1 Session 5","marks":1}],"section":"try"}],"practice":[{"id":"week16.16.1.p2","kind":"mcq","q":"SECOND CHANCE (motion graphs). A cyclist accelerates uniformly from rest to 6.0 m s^−1^ in 4.0 s, then rides at a steady 6.0 m s^−1^ for 6.0 s. The total distance travelled is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["12 m","36 m","48 m","60 m"],"answer":"C","explanation":"(1). Area under the v–t graph: triangle ⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 4.0\\times 6.0=12\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫, plus rectangle ⟪6.0\\times 6.0=36\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫; total 48 m. D (60 m) treats the whole 10 s as 6.0 m s^−1^.","section":"practice"}],"exit":[{"id":"week16.16.1.p3","kind":"mcq","q":"SECOND CHANCE (circuits). Two 8 Ω resistors are connected in parallel. This pair is connected in series with a 2 Ω resistor across a 12 V supply. The current from the supply is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["6.0 A","2.0 A","1.2 A","0.67 A"],"answer":"B","explanation":"(1). Parallel pair: ⟪8\\times 8\\div (8+8)=4\\,\\Omega⟫. Total ⟪=4+2=6\\,\\Omega⟫. ⟪I=12\\div 6=2.0\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫. D (0.67 A) adds all three in series (18 Ω).","section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week16.16.1.w1","kind":"self","q":"How many items are in Paper 01 and how long do you get? About how long per item?","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"60 items in 1 h 15 min (75 min): about 75 seconds each.","points":[{"text":"60 items in 1 h 15 min (75 min): about 75 seconds each.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week15.r1"},{"id":"week16.16.1.w2","kind":"self","q":"Name the two classic traps in a Q = It question.","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"Using minutes instead of seconds; and dividing instead of multiplying.","points":[{"text":"Using minutes instead of seconds; and dividing instead of multiplying.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week15.r2"},{"id":"week16.16.1.w3","kind":"self","q":"What happens to A and Z in alpha decay and in beta decay?","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"Alpha: ⟪A-4⟫, ⟪Z-2⟫. Beta: A unchanged, ⟪Z+1⟫.","points":[{"text":"Alpha: ⟪A-4⟫, ⟪Z-2⟫. Beta: A unchanged, ⟪Z+1⟫.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week15.r3"},{"id":"week16.16.1.w4","kind":"self","q":"Two resistors of 6 Ω and 3 Ω are in parallel. Combined resistance?","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"⟪2\\,\\Omega \\ (1/R=1/6+1/3=1/2)⟫.","points":[{"text":"⟪2\\,\\Omega \\ (1/R=1/6+1/3=1/2)⟫.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week15.r4"},{"id":"week16.16.1.w5","kind":"self","q":"Where do the units go in a results table, and what scale values should you avoid on a graph?","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"In the column heading (quantity / unit); avoid awkward scales such as 3 or 7 units per square.","points":[{"text":"In the column heading (quantity / unit); avoid awkward scales such as 3 or 7 units per square.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week15.r5"}],"n":122},{"id":"week16.16.2","kind":"unit","unit":"16.2","title":"Last-week revision plan (18–25 Jan)","titleText":"Last-week revision plan (18–25 Jan)","goals":[{"code":"P01","text":"Sit a full Paper 01 in 75 minutes, mark it, find my weak topics and follow a final revision plan"}],"codes":["P01"],"words":[{"term":"Item","def":"One multiple-choice question in Paper 01."},{"term":"Stem","def":"The question part of an item."},{"term":"Key","def":"The correct option."},{"term":"Checkpoint","def":"A time by which you should have reached a certain item."},{"term":"Alternate candidate","def":"A private candidate who sits Paper 032 instead of submitting SBA marks."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"In the last week you are not learning new physics. You are **closing gaps** and getting **fast and accurate**. Follow this plan day by day and tick each day off."},{"type":"table","headers":["Day","Focus","What to do","Time","Done ✓"],"rows":[["**Mon 18 Jan**","Mark the mock","Fill in both tables in Unit 16.1. Redo every wrong item without looking. Start your Mistake Log list.","60 min",""],["**Tue 19 Jan**","Weakest section (1st)","Open the matching workbook: re-read the notes for the topics you missed, learn its Formula & Fact Card and Common Mistakes, redo its Exam-Level MCQs.","60 min",""],["**Wed 20 Jan**","Weakest section (2nd)","Same method for your second-weakest section. Then 10 min on Section E facts: α/β/γ, A and Z changes, half-life.","60 min",""],["**Thu 21 Jan**","Full timed paper","A real past Paper 01 from your booklet: 60 items in 75 minutes, one sitting. Mark it and log the errors.","90 min",""],["**Fri 22 Jan**","Fix and drill","Fix Thursday's errors. Do the Week 15 Paper 01 Drill practice items for the sections where you still lose marks.","60 min",""],["**Sat 23 Jan**","Second timed practice","Another real Paper 01 (or the Week 15 30-item mixed test, 38 min). Mark it. Re-read your whole Mistake Log.","75 min",""],["**Sun 24 Jan**","Light review ONLY","Formula & Fact Cards and definitions, 30 min. NO new topics. Pack your bag (checklist below). Early night.","30 min",""],["**Mon 25 Jan**","EXAM DAY","Paper 01 at **9:00**. Breakfast; arrive by 8:30. (Paper 032 is at 13:00 ONLY if you are an alternate candidate.)","1 h 15",""]]},{"type":"tip","text":"(1) minutes not changed to seconds; (2) °C used instead of kelvin in gas laws; (3) kW and W mixed up; (4) extension confused with length; (5) a ratio put upside down (transformer turns, moments, lens magnification).","label":"THE FIVE TRAPS THAT COST THE MOST MARKS"},{"type":"h","text":"Exam-day checklist"},{"type":"bullets","items":["Candidate slip / ID, and the start time (9:00) and venue written down.","Two sharpened **HB pencils**, a clean eraser, a sharpener, a pen and a ruler.","A silent, non-programmable **calculator** with a working battery.","A watch (not a phone) so you can keep to your checkpoints.","Water and a light breakfast before you leave home."]},{"type":"h","text":"In the exam room"},{"type":"steps","items":["Read each stem fully. **Underline** key words (NOT, MOST, LEAST) and units (min, °C, kW, mA).","Cover the options and try to answer first; then find your answer among the options.","If two options look right, eliminate the others and pick the more complete one.","If an item takes longer than 2 minutes, mark it, guess, move on and come back at the end.","Keep to the checkpoints: item 20 by 9:25, item 40 by 9:50, item 60 by 10:10.","In the last 5 minutes check that **every row has exactly one** shaded box."]},{"type":"remember","text":"No new topics after Saturday. The night before, sleep is worth more than one more hour of revision."}],"examples":[]},"tryit":[{"id":"week16.16.2.p1","kind":"num","q":"Paper 01 starts at 9:00. If you spend 75 seconds on each item, at what time should you finish item 40?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪40\\times 75\\,\\mathrm{s}=3000\\,\\mathrm{s}=50\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫, so 9:50 (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪40\\times 75\\,\\mathrm{s}=3000\\,\\mathrm{s}=50\\,\\mathrm{min}⟫, so 9:50","marks":1}],"num":{"value":50,"sf":1,"unit":"","unitTex":""},"section":"try"}],"practice":[{"id":"week16.16.2.p2","kind":"mcq","q":"TRAP CHECK (minutes). A current of 0.20 A flows for 10 minutes. The charge that flows is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["0.020 C","2.0 C","50 C","120 C"],"answer":"D","explanation":"(1). Q = It ⟪=0.20\\times (10\\times 60)=0.20\\times 600=120\\,\\mathrm{C}⟫. B (2.0 C) forgets to change minutes into seconds.","section":"practice"}],"exit":[{"id":"week16.16.2.p3","kind":"mcq","q":"TRAP CHECK (kelvin). A gas at constant volume has a pressure of 90 kPa at 27 °C. It is heated to 127 °C. Its new pressure is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["67.5 kPa","90 kPa","120 kPa","423 kPa"],"answer":"C","explanation":"(1). Pressure law with kelvin: ⟪p_{2}=90\\times 400\\div 300=120\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫. D (423 kPa) uses °C: ⟪90\\times 127\\div 27⟫.","section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week16.16.2.w1","kind":"mcq","q":"SECOND CHANCE (circuits). Two 8 Ω resistors are connected in parallel. This pair is connected in series with a 2 Ω resistor across a 12 V supply. The current from the supply is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["6.0 A","2.0 A","1.2 A","0.67 A"],"answer":"B","explanation":"(1). Parallel pair: ⟪8\\times 8\\div (8+8)=4\\,\\Omega⟫. Total ⟪=4+2=6\\,\\Omega⟫. ⟪I=12\\div 6=2.0\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫. D (0.67 A) adds all three in series (18 Ω).","section":"warmup","src":"week16.16.1.p3"},{"id":"week16.16.2.w2","kind":"mcq","q":"SECOND CHANCE (motion graphs). A cyclist accelerates uniformly from rest to 6.0 m s^−1^ in 4.0 s, then rides at a steady 6.0 m s^−1^ for 6.0 s. The total distance travelled is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["12 m","36 m","48 m","60 m"],"answer":"C","explanation":"(1). Area under the v–t graph: triangle ⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 4.0\\times 6.0=12\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫, plus rectangle ⟪6.0\\times 6.0=36\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫; total 48 m. D (60 m) treats the whole 10 s as 6.0 m s^−1^.","section":"warmup","src":"week16.16.1.p2"}],"n":123}]};
